**My Answer:**

C++ does not have built-in garbage collection like Java or Python, but you can achieve automatic memory management for linked lists using smart pointers, specifically `std::unique_ptr` for singly linked lists. This way, when the list or its nodes go out of scope, memory is automatically reclaimed.

Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

struct Node {
    int data;
    std::unique_ptr<Node> next;
    Node(int val) : data(val), next(nullptr) {}
};

class LinkedList {
    std::unique_ptr<Node> head;
public:
    void push_front(int val) {
        auto new_node = std::make_unique<Node>(val);
        new_node->next = std::move(head);
        head = std::move(new_node);
    }

    void print() const {
        Node* curr = head.get();
        while (curr) {
            std::cout << curr->data << " -> ";
            curr = curr->next.get();
        }
        std::cout << "nullptr\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList list;
    list.push_front(3);
    list.push_front(2);
    list.push_front(1);
    list.print();
    // Memory is automatically reclaimed when list goes out of scope
}
```

**Explanation:**  
- Each node owns its successor via `unique_ptr`, so when a node is destroyed, its next node is also destroyed recursively.
- No manual `delete` is needed.
- This is the idiomatic way to achieve "automatic garbage collection" for linked lists in modern C++.

If you need a doubly linked list or shared ownership, you would use `std::shared_ptr` and `std::weak_ptr`, but for singly linked lists, `unique_ptr` is preferred.

