The user prompt describes a scenario where there are N steady-state points on a plane, and for any arbitrary point in space, we want to estimate its corresponding steady-state by projecting it perpendicularly onto the baseline formed by connecting the first and last steady-state points. The intersection point (the foot of the perpendicular) on the baseline is the estimated steady-state.

There are two possible interpretations:
1. The steady-state is the continuous projection point on the baseline (if the baseline represents a continuum of steady-states).
2. The steady-state is one of the discrete N points, so after projection, we map the projection to the nearest or corresponding discrete steady-state point.

**MATLAB code for both interpretations:**

**A. Continuous projection (returns the foot of the perpendicular):**
```matlab
function Q = estimateSteadyStateProjection(steadyPoints, P)
    % steadyPoints: N x 2 matrix, first and last row define the baseline
    % P: 1 x 2 vector, the point to estimate
    A = steadyPoints(1, :);
    B = steadyPoints(end, :);
    AB = B - A;
    AP = P - A;
    t = dot(AP, AB) / dot(AB, AB);
    % Optionally clamp t to [0,1] if only the segment is valid
    t = max(0, min(1, t));
    Q = A + t * AB;
end
```

**B. Discrete mapping (returns the nearest steady-state point):**
```matlab
function idx = estimateSteadyStateIndex(steadyPoints, P)
    % steadyPoints: N x 2 matrix
    % P: 1 x 2 vector
    A = steadyPoints(1, :);
    B = steadyPoints(end, :);
    AB = B - A;
    AP = P - A;
    t = dot(AP, AB) / dot(AB, AB);
    t = max(0, min(1, t));
    N = size(steadyPoints, 1);
    idx = round(1 + (N-1)*t);
    idx = max(1, min(N, idx));
end
```

**Usage example:**
```matlab
steadyPoints = [0 0; 2 1; 4 2; 6 3];
P = [3, 5];
Q = estimateSteadyStateProjection(steadyPoints, P)
idx = estimateSteadyStateIndex(steadyPoints, P)
```

