To solve this problem, we need to determine if there exists an integer point (not already in the set) that, when added, becomes the closest point to every existing point in the set.

**Key Observations:**
- For each point x in the set, its closest neighbor is at distance d (where d is the minimum distance to any other point in the set).
- For the new point p to be the closest to x, the distance |x - p| must be less than or equal to d, and strictly less than the distance to any other point (since all other points are at least d away).
- However, since the original set is sorted and all points are distinct, for each x, d is the minimum of the distances to its immediate neighbors.

**Approach:**
1. For each point x, compute d = min(x - prev, next - x) (with care for endpoints).
2. For each x, the new point p must satisfy |x - p| < d (strictly less, since otherwise the original neighbor is equally close).
3. The intersection of all such intervals for all x gives the possible positions for p.
4. If there is any integer p in this intersection that is not already in the set, output YES; otherwise, NO.

**Implementation:**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <unordered_set>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while (t--) {
        int n;
        cin >> n;
        vector<int> x(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cin >> x[i];

        int L = -1e9, R = 1e9;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            int d;
            if (i == 0) d = x[1] - x[0];
            else if (i == n - 1) d = x[n - 1] - x[n - 2];
            else d = min(x[i] - x[i - 1], x[i + 1] - x[i]);
            // Strictly less than d
            int low = x[i] - d + 1;
            int high = x[i] + d - 1;
            L = max(L, low);
